WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.780
A cell should divide only when it is told to.

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.480
So every signaling pathway has an on-step and an off-step.

3
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.500
The signal flips a switch on, and the off-step flips it back.

4
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.300
Now suppose the switch sticks.

5
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.680
The cell divides on its own.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.080
There are two ways to make that happen: jam an on-step or remove an off-step.

7
00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:31.403
First, jam the on-step.

8
00:00:32.223 --> 00:00:34.483
Ras is on while it holds GTP.

9
00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:41.863
Normally a helper called a GAP makes Ras break down that GTP, and the switch turns

10
00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:47.983
off. One changed amino acid, as in Ras G12V, stops this.

11
00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:52.503
Ras cannot break down its GTP, even with a GAP.

12
00:00:52.503 --> 00:00:57.023
A gene that can turn a cell cancerous is an oncogene.

13
00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:00.823
Second, remove the off-step.

14
00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:04.783
Neurofibromin is a GAP for Ras.

15
00:01:05.423 --> 00:01:07.003
It is the off-step.

16
00:01:08.123 --> 00:01:12.163
Lose neurofibromin, and Ras-GTP is not cleared.

17
00:01:12.963 --> 00:01:15.083
No on-step is jammed here.

18
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:17.083
An off-step is missing.

19
00:01:17.923 --> 00:01:23.063
A gene whose loss lets cells grow when they should not is a tumour suppressor.

20
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.677
Now count the copies.

21
00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:31.077
You carry two copies of nearly every gene, one from each parent.

22
00:01:32.137 --> 00:01:35.197
Suppose one copy of the Ras gene is changed.

23
00:01:35.937 --> 00:01:39.097
The altered protein signals without being told to.

24
00:01:39.797 --> 00:01:43.417
The normal protein from the other copy cannot stop it.

25
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:44.897
So the cell divides.

26
00:01:45.977 --> 00:01:49.037
This is a gain of function, and it is dominant.

27
00:01:49.777 --> 00:01:52.197
It shows even when the other copy is normal.

28
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:55.137
An off-switch is different.

29
00:01:56.017 --> 00:02:00.177
One good copy usually makes enough protein to do the job.

30
00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:04.697
Lose one copy of the gene, and the off-step still works.

31
00:02:05.637 --> 00:02:08.737
Both copies must go before the off-step fails.

32
00:02:09.977 --> 00:02:13.517
Alfred Knudson proposed that a tumour takes two hits.

33
00:02:14.277 --> 00:02:17.837
The two hits turned out to be the two copies of one gene.

34
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:23.370
So a stuck switch follows two rules, whichever way it sticks.

35
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:28.230
A jammed on-step acts when one copy is altered.

36
00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.670
A lost off-step usually needs both copies gone.

37
00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.990
Gain acts alone. Loss needs both.
